unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-365879/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-365879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "e1ef5a4d-b5be-5746-b695-eac02235aa74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "d1439cf5-3c64-593d-9ac7-2a47568830a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f58fe6e2-5c8f-5be1-850a-27e8e1b00d20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "90ac626c-ab61-5f42-afc5-9d5f347206dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "db7bcabe-194a-5f07-bd12-33d451ce9bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3305e770-a298-53a6-88c6-a301ddcdf6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-365880/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-365880/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-365880/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-365880/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-365880/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-365880/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-365880" :question/id #uuid "eb7e51d1-4895-5640-8b7f-9992fc6a947d" :question/math-academy-id "q-365880" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{y}{2}+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-365880/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-365880"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection/9f6bfd6fa85d96298a99db901d9b73f609ef64ac0bcf5fbdc4a7ac6d3b733558" :answer/id #uuid "d3584d84-bea1-5249-929f-abcbd126cbac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "2f9e35f5-c3bb-5378-8b76-a0c4d847a0d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "008a0b3b-5b2f-541a-bb2d-59af0bd86ee7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "d1dd8703-0ae3-50c7-9f7a-818d4125dafd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" :answer/id #uuid "a6d5a3de-0a74-5caf-845a-fbdd89e04c30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4290f29c-4976-5702-a085-00b0852a2988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162154/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer-field/choices ["q-162154/selection/9f6bfd6fa85d96298a99db901d9b73f609ef64ac0bcf5fbdc4a7ac6d3b733558" "q-162154/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-162154/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-162154/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" "q-162154/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162154" :question/id #uuid "236061fb-0f20-5446-8754-3d5fb811ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-162154" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x-y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{6}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162154/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "00815175-e424-55ac-9a61-00315846fc49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "dc64109e-4907-5b84-8276-84dbf454211e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "03f4ffbb-505a-5d71-90b0-7212c9a96a83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "6382e7b6-3bd6-57c6-a4ad-050d0d353fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "5e1ac034-5dd4-5972-b196-64538f826278" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "36959d77-1a54-57f2-9b6d-b2116a11c76a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162540/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-162540/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-162540/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-162540/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-162540/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-162540/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162540" :question/id #uuid "3200d2a8-1cae-51d8-8794-e7d6274b1d00" :question/math-academy-id "q-162540" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+2y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+1,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-x-2y-1+2y+4x+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162540/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162540"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162546/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "fc569fbc-