efined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{11}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162546/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection/8106374aed153a327a68ce5bcf6708f5921c9920659df51b755dc8bf3bca88b4" :answer/id #uuid "f6915ef1-f87c-5002-b38e-c005c3880616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection/c6aee8d6d50719f300760eb2495148ceeb4788aba7a3722d82e109afb19d07de" :answer/id #uuid "c01105be-2fe8-55f9-875a-39151c2fdfdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "04e037c9-6da9-55dd-beb3-9501ffb6606a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "be456f7b-7401-5b12-8145-b88431667184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection/b7fab282fb64e382abe17afa95b63b5974447653091ee82823065058b9fe02d2" :answer/id #uuid "8e23539d-d6fa-5b4d-99f5-6819d39d73c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e028a01c-5683-5d1d-a1e0-a752115fb27c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161194/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-161194/selection/8106374aed153a327a68ce5bcf6708f5921c9920659df51b755dc8bf3bca88b4" "q-161194/selection/c6aee8d6d50719f300760eb2495148ceeb4788aba7a3722d82e109afb19d07de" "q-161194/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-161194/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" "q-161194/selection/b7fab282fb64e382abe17afa95b63b5974447653091ee82823065058b9fe02d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161194" :question/id #uuid "f8620aa7-548d-5834-a6eb-0fd02b29a086" :question/math-academy-id "q-161194" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2y-x-5$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161194/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161194"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "78c42c5d-766a-52c2-8bbe-47f3f6306cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "e35aea69-ad41-5c21-8f1e-7c0fee78b343" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "84dc99df-bfaa-59f6-bd26-46bbe29439aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "d8546ba5-f972-5922-9adf-123a2b9fde37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "10b9d577-e816-5b85-bbec-07fdc3b5bbe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "61b02866-e594-5d23-bdf8-42482fcd61a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162147/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-162147/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-162147/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-162147/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-162147/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-162147/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162147" :question/id #uuid "712b23f7-2acb-542c-b6c5-1a7ef8fe14eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162147" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162147/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162147"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12356"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/assets/35bcfa2242051bed034ac3343c1a6d67f94916d4267030f726e2bd27cbbe02f1.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12384"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12454"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||\\mathbf r_x \\times \\mathbf r_y|| =3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
